"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[11],{1011:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"strings","title":"Strings","overviewSimple":"Strings are like word necklaces where each bead is a letter. In Java, strings are sequences of characters used to store text, like names, messages, or any kind of text data.","overviewTechnical":["In Java, a String is an object that represents a sequence of characters.","Strings are immutable, meaning once created, their values cannot be changed.","The String class provides methods for comparison, searching, substring extraction, and manipulation.","String literals are stored in a special memory area called the String pool for memory efficiency.","Java also provides mutable string classes like StringBuilder and StringBuffer for frequent modifications.","Strings can be concatenated using the + operator or the concat() method.","Strings can be compared using equals() for value comparison and == for reference comparison.","Common methods include length(), charAt(), substring(), indexOf(), toUpperCase(), toLowerCase(), trim(), and split().","StringBuilder is faster than String for repeated modifications due to its mutable nature.","StringBuffer is thread-safe, making it suitable for multithreaded environments."],"story":"Imagine a magical bracelet maker that strings together letters to form names or messages. Once a bracelet is made, it cannot be altered \u2014 you have to make a new one. Java Strings work in the same way; they hold text that is immutable.","realWorldAnalogy":"Strings are like printed labels \u2014 once printed, the text on the label cannot change. If you need to change it, you make a new label.","keyPoints":["Strings are objects, not primitive types","Immutable by default","Stored in the String pool for memory efficiency","Use StringBuilder/StringBuffer for mutable strings","Methods available for manipulation and searching","Concatenate with + or concat()","Compare with equals() for content, == for reference","Useful for text processing and formatting"],"exampleSimple":"String name = \\"Alice\\";\\nString greeting = \\"Hello, \\" + name;\\nSystem.out.println(greeting);","exampleTechnical":["public class StringDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        String s1 = \\"Hello\\";\\n        String s2 = \\"World\\";\\n        String s3 = s1 + \\" \\" + s2;\\n\\n        System.out.println(\\"Concatenated: \\" + s3);\\n        System.out.println(\\"Length: \\" + s3.length());\\n        System.out.println(\\"Character at 1: \\" + s3.charAt(1));\\n        System.out.println(\\"Substring(0,5): \\" + s3.substring(0,5));\\n    }\\n}","public class StringBuilderDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"Hello\\");\\n        sb.append(\\" World\\");\\n        sb.insert(6, \\"Java \\");\\n        sb.replace(0, 5, \\"Hi\\");\\n\\n        System.out.println(sb.toString());\\n\\n        // Reverse\\n        sb.reverse();\\n        System.out.println(\\"Reversed: \\" + sb.toString());\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["Strings are widely used in Java applications for user input, file processing, logging, and network communication.","Immutability of Strings improves security, thread-safety, and performance for constants.","For performance-critical code with frequent modifications, prefer StringBuilder over String."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Use String literals for constant text","Use + for simple concatenation","Always check string length before accessing characters"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Use StringBuilder or StringBuffer for repeated modifications","Use equals() for comparing string content instead of ==","Leverage built-in string methods to simplify code","Avoid creating unnecessary string objects in loops","Use trim() to clean user input before processing"]}}')}}]);